{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1572430268,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI3NTUzMzM4MDE="},"added":["question"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1572430268,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI3NTUzMzM4MDM="},"added":["UX"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1572434241,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0Nzg1MjcyMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3845#issuecomment-547852722"},"message":"I guess for Yoda it should be ok","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1572435777,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0Nzg2MTYzMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3845#issuecomment-547861633"},"message":"Hm. This can be quite surprising and it's unclear to me, what this name and purpose has to do with YODA.\nAlso: How is it more easy to know that this particular config procedure happens to recognize a particular name for such a place than to just add `tmp/*` to `.gitignore`?","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1572435777,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMwMTA1MzUzNw=="},"target":"71b2ee010058c5fb8087593c714dbb2ee732d06dbaf6ecc075dca68483105008","message":"Hm. This can be quite surprising and it's unclear to me, what this name and purpose has to do with YODA.\nAlso: How is it more easy to know that this particular config procedure happens to recognize a particular name for such a place than to just add `tmp/*` to `.gitignore` yourself?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1572624543,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0ODg0NjU3Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3845#issuecomment-548846572"},"message":"FWIW my reaction matched @bpoldrack's.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1572625803,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0ODg1MzkyMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3845#issuecomment-548853923"},"message":"Hmm, maybe we have different concepts of these config procedures. From my POV, this procedure does nothing but implement a set of conventions. My proposal was asking to add another convention to an existing set. YODA was invented to promote a particular dataset layout, having a place within the dataset that serves as scratch space seems like a good addition -- given that the existence of such a places (heterogeneously named for the lack of a convention) prompted this issue.\n\nIf the problem is \"why not configure it yourself\" then my question is: what is the point in configuring `code/` and arbitrarily naming it such?\n\nIf the problem is why YODA then it is fine with me to just copy the yoda procedure and have another one with a different name. In this case, I'd prefer to adjust to handbook to refer to this alternative procedure, as within its scope (and the associate course) people learn about useful conventions.\n\nWhat am I missing?","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1572625803,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMwMjUxMzU5Mw=="},"target":"b29ae7d9bb06940ec63cb6749627d9d75d4c9b68af9508b0eaa720faa7ca4d0b","message":"Hmm, maybe we have different concepts of these config procedures. From my POV, this procedure does nothing but implement a set of conventions. My proposal was asking to add another convention to an existing set. YODA was invented to promote a particular dataset layout, having a place within the dataset that serves as scratch space seems like a good addition -- given that the existence of such a places (heterogeneously named for the lack of a convention) prompted this issue.\n\nIf the problem is \"why not configure it yourself\" then my question is: what is the point in configuring `code/` and arbitrarily naming it such, in an arbitrarily named procedure? ;-)\n\nIf the problem is why YODA then it is fine with me to just copy the yoda procedure and have another one with a different name. In this case, I'd prefer to adjust to handbook to refer to this alternative procedure, as within its scope (and the associate course) people learn about useful conventions.\n\nWhat am I missing?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1572632520,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0ODg5Nzc0NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3845#issuecomment-548897744"},"message":"\u003e having a place within the dataset that serves as scratch space seems like a good addition -- given that the existence of such a places (heterogeneously named for the lack of a convention) prompted this issue.\n\nI don't see this heterogeneity as a problem that needs solving.  Why does using a common directory matter for content that is untracked?  I think that's the core of the issue for me.\n\nThat said, I don't think ignoring \"/tmp/\" or \"tmp/\" has much potential to cause harm.  The worst case scenario I can come up with is that an inexperienced git user puts content in \"tmp/\" for whatever reason and doesn't realize that this directory is being ignored and that these changes aren't being saved.  The more likely scenario is just that a user doesn't prefer the setting and modifies `.gitignore` to their liking or doesn't use `cfg_yoda`.  So I don't have a strong objection.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1572691577,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0OTAzMjA0MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3845#issuecomment-549032040"},"message":"\u003e I don't see this heterogeneity as a problem that needs solving.\n\nTrue, but it is not the heterogeneity that I am concerned with. I want to be able to promote an \"always clean\" workflow, where there is nothing untracked cluttering a dataset. At the same time, I want to promote making more smaller commits, because they tend to be more comprehensible and are also easier to squash into one, than it is to take a bulk commit apart. In such a scenario, one frequently needs a place to put things that need/must not be tracked. That implies that anyone, more or less immediately needs to create directory that can serve as a tmp folder, and gitignore it.\nOf course this could be left to each user, but inexperienced users might move that tmp folder outside the dataset. This in turn increases the likelihood that code tracked in a dataset references locations outside the dataset -- which might impact portability.\n\nSo in general, I am looking for the optimal trade-off between setup complexity and \nnovice users intuitively doing the right thing from the very start -- without requiring them to know all the tricks first.\n\n\u003e The worst case scenario I can come up with is that an inexperienced git user puts content in \"tmp/\" for whatever reason and doesn't realize that this directory is being ignored and that these changes aren't being saved.\n\nThat is an issue, I agree. What about going one step further, and not just adding gitignore configuration, but actually creating a tmp folder and putting an empty `NO_VERSION_CONTROL_HERE` file into it? Of course this wouldn't help later clones of such a dataset, but I'd argue that once you install a dataset from somewhere, you are less novice than after your first `create`.\n\nIt is not perfect, but I would be satisfied if the chances for harm are tiny and the potential for good is non-zero.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1572708894,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0OTA1NDU1NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3845#issuecomment-549054555"},"message":"\u003e \u003e I don't see this heterogeneity as a problem that needs solving.\n\u003e \n\u003e True, but it is not the heterogeneity that I am concerned with. I want to be able to promote an \"always clean\" workflow, where there is nothing untracked cluttering a dataset. At the same time, I want to promote making more smaller commits, because they tend to be more comprehensible and are also easier to squash into one, than it is to take a bulk commit apart. In such a scenario, one frequently needs a place to put things that need/must not be tracked. That implies that anyone, more or less immediately needs to create directory that can serve as a tmp folder, and gitignore it.\n\nInteresting.\n\nReading that I can't help but think that this problem is the reason that git's index exists, but the index is a concept that datalad hides away.  I think that points to the fact that my reaction really shouldn't count for much here.  I'm not the target audience, and, while I do try to look at things from that point of view, I'm clearly failing on this one.\n\n\u003e \u003e The worst case scenario I can come up with is that an inexperienced git user puts content in \"tmp/\" for whatever reason and doesn't realize that this directory is being ignored and that these changes aren't being saved.\n\u003e \n\u003e That is an issue, I agree. What about going one step further, and not just adding gitignore configuration, but actually creating a tmp folder and putting an empty `NO_VERSION_CONTROL_HERE` file into it?\n\nYeah, sounds fine.  Perhaps just a README.md that explains the purpose of the directory, like what's already done for `code/`, would be sufficient.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1572710303,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0OTA1NjQ1Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3845#issuecomment-549056456"},"message":"I am with @mih on this. ;-)\n\nHaving said that, adding anything to .gitignore should be done indeed with the caution, since it could directly effect reproducibility of the runs. I even wonder if we could somehow inform user after `run` that X files changes were ignored because those were ignored. But that might be too costly. May be at least to alert that such ignored paths (as tmp/) exist","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1572774446,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0OTEyMDQxOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3845#issuecomment-549120419"},"message":"If we want to issue a valid warning, the only way that I see is to have a dedicated `ls-files --exclude-standard --ignored` run (per dataset) before and after a `run` to check them for differences. Just looking at local `.gitignore` files won't give us the full picture, and IMHO issuing some warnings, but not reliable enough that they are actually trustworthy would be more problematic than not having any, and merely documenting that ignored files can be an issue for reproducibility.","files":null}]}